WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.960
It seems like everybody nowadays&nbsp;
wants to "reach fluency in three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.400
months" or "learn a language in 90&nbsp;
days". But is that even possible? Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:12.060
is it just another Internet myth?&nbsp;
The answer might surprise you!

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.500
Hey, I am Luca Lampariello, I am a language&nbsp;
coach who speaks 14 languages. and on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:20.700
channel I help people learn foreign languages&nbsp;
more efficiently, faster and in a fun way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.600
So, can you learn a language&nbsp;
in 3 months? Let’s find out!

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:29.040
Search Google or YouTube for nearly anything&nbsp;
related to language learning nowadays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:33.180
and you'll find all sorts of videos,&nbsp;
blog posts and content about "learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:37.380
a language fast". It's not uncommon to&nbsp;
see people claiming that it's possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.520
to reach fluency in a few months,&nbsp;
a few days, or even 24 hours!

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:46.980
It's gotten so bad that one of the most&nbsp;
common "fast" goals—reaching fluency in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:51.300
3 months—sounds downright slow compared&nbsp;
to a lot of the more outrageous examples.

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.360
But in a world where everyone wants fluency fast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:59.940
is it even possible to learn a language at that&nbsp;
speed? Can you really become fluent in 90 days?

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.620
If you ask me, there's some truth&nbsp;
to it. But that truth comes with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.440
a lot of qualifiers, and a lot of caveats.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.420
First of all, it's impossible to determine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.980
if someone has "reached fluency" until&nbsp;
you define what "fluency" actually is.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:17.940
Believe it or not, "fluency"&nbsp;
is an incredibly vague word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:19.920
with no universally agreed-upon definition.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.820
Even within the online language learning&nbsp;
community, everyone seems to define "fluent"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:28.320
in different ways. Because of this, when someone&nbsp;
tells me that “I want to be fluent in Russian in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.940
3 months”, I have no idea what they're actually&nbsp;
aiming to accomplish. "Fluency" here could mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:37.380
anything from "basic conversational skills"&nbsp;
to "sounding like an educated native speaker",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:41.580
and everything in between. That's&nbsp;
a massive range in skill levels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:46.260
and only a few of those are remotely&nbsp;
feasible in a 90-day period.

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:49.920
So in order to determine if it's possible&nbsp;
to reach fluency in three months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:53.700
we need to settle on a definition&nbsp;
of "fluency", and go from there.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.500
To me, being fluent means being comfortable&nbsp;
in expressing yourself in most situations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:02.340
as well as comfortably understanding&nbsp;
what is being said to you.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:07.080
In short, I believe fluency is about&nbsp;
familiarity and comfort with a language.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:14.160
That's a good start, but to make things even&nbsp;
clearer, let's also define what fluency is NOT.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:19.560
To me, fluency does not mean speaking&nbsp;
perfectly like a native. Nor does it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.500
mean understanding everything you read or hear.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:28.080
That right there is a significant departure from&nbsp;
the concept of fluency held by a lot of people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.940
particularly non-language learners. It's not&nbsp;
uncommon to hear people use "perfectly" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:37.500
"fluently" interchangeably when talking about&nbsp;
someone else's language skills. But again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.840
that's why we're defining our terms.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.740
To make my definition of fluency even more&nbsp;
concrete, I'd like to use an objective set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:48.060
of standards, one that is used by language&nbsp;
learners and professionals all over the world:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:54.240
the Common European Framework of Reference&nbsp;
for Languages, or CEFR for short. Put simply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.880
the CEFR is a set of guidelines for describing&nbsp;
achievement and ability in language learners.&nbsp;

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.200
As part of these guidelines, there are&nbsp;
six "reference levels" that describe the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.840
full range of skill levels in a foreign language.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:15.180
From lowest to highest, these levels&nbsp;
are A1, A2, B1, B2, C1, and C2.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.420
The full details of this framework&nbsp;
are beyond the scope of this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:22.680
but if you'd like to learn more about it, please&nbsp;
check the description box for more information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:27.900
For now, though, I just want to touch&nbsp;
upon the B2 level, which is the CEFR&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:31.740
level that I believe most accurately&nbsp;
corresponds to my definition of fluency.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:35.580
According to the Council of Europe, a&nbsp;
language learner able to speak at a B2 level:

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:40.020
Can interact with a degree of fluency and&nbsp;
spontaneity that makes regular interaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:44.580
with native speakers quite possible, and
Can take an active part in discussion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:48.900
in familiar contexts, accounting&nbsp;
for and sustaining [their] views.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:51.540
That, in a nutshell, is what fluency means to me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:56.640
A comfortable level of language skill that&nbsp;
corresponds to a B2 on the CEFR scale.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.380
So, with that in mind, let's take another&nbsp;
shot at answering our key question:

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:05.100
Is it possible to reach fluency in three months?

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.760
Well, I've got good news, and bad news.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:14.040
I'll start with the bad news: no, it's&nbsp;
not possible to reach fluency (again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:18.180
a B2 level) in three months. I've met&nbsp;
a lot of language learners in my life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.180 --> 00:04:22.200
and I've never met anyone who has managed&nbsp;
to become fluent in that time period.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:27.060
But this is why defining fluency is&nbsp;
so important. I say it's not possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:31.680
but that's according to my own definition.&nbsp;
If I were using other definitions of fluency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.360
then my answer might be different.

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.440
Anyway, enough with the bad&nbsp;
news. What about the good news?

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:41.760
Well the good news is that even&nbsp;
though I believe it's impossible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:45.360
to become fluent in three months - no&nbsp;
matter the language - it is certainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:50.040
possible to make a lot of progress&nbsp;
TOWARDS fluency in that time period!

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:54.780
Believe me, I've met many, many people&nbsp;
who managed to learn a heck of a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:58.380
of language in a three-month time&nbsp;
span. More than you can imagine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.800
in fact! And at the end of those&nbsp;
three months, those same people were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:05.880
well-poised to make a solid push towards&nbsp;
fluency in the coming months, or years.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:10.380
So while I don't believe fluency is&nbsp;
a worthwhile target in three months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:15.000
I certainly believe it's worthwhile to learn&nbsp;
as much as you can in that same time period.

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:19.860
But that leads us to a different question:&nbsp;
if you can't reach fluency in three months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:22.440
how much CAN you learn in that time?

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:24.960
Returning to our trusty CEFR scale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:29.580
I believe the answer lies at the level just&nbsp;
below B2, which, naturally, is called B1.

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:34.980
According to the CEFR, the B1 is the first&nbsp;
level where a learner can be considered an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:39.180
"independent user" of the language. Personally,&nbsp;
I like to refer to this level of skill as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:44.760
"conversational fluency"—you're not fully fluent&nbsp;
yet, but you're comfortable enough to speak and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:49.500
interact with others in a relatively smooth&nbsp;
way, on a variety of common, everyday topics.

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:52.860
Diving a little deeper into the&nbsp;
CEFRs definition of the B1 level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:57.360
we learn that someone at a B1 level:
Can understand the main points of clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:02.640
standard input on familiar matters regularly&nbsp;
encountered in work, school, leisure, etc.&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:06.360
Can deal with most situations&nbsp;
likely to arise while traveling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:10.800
in an area where the language is spoken.
Can produce simple connected text on topics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:15.600
which are familiar or of personal interest.
Can describe experiences and events, dreams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.100
hopes &amp; ambitions and briefly give reasons&nbsp;
and explanations for opinions and plans.&nbsp;

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:25.080
In most cases, I believe such a level is entirely&nbsp;
attainable by most dedicated language learners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:29.880
so long as a few conditions are met.
These conditions (in no particular order) are:&nbsp;

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:31.080
Time on task&nbsp;

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:35.460
The "distance" between your&nbsp;
native and target languages, and&nbsp;

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:38.940
Life circumstances that are&nbsp;
favorable for language learning

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:42.300
Let's talk about each of&nbsp;
these a little more in depth:

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:43.860
First, "time on task":

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:49.800
Three months is a little over 90 days. That might&nbsp;
sound like a lot of time to get things done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:54.180
but if you're an absolute beginner who wants&nbsp;
to make a serious push towards a B1 level,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:58.140
then you're going to have to devote a huge&nbsp;
chunk of that time to language learning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:02.940
each and every day. And I'm not talking one&nbsp;
or two hours; to reach your three-month goal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:07.440
you'll have to spend anywhere from three to&nbsp;
eight hours a day learning. That's practically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:11.160
a full-time job! If you don't have that&nbsp;
amount of time to devote to your target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:14.100
language during those three months,&nbsp;
it's simply not going to happen.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:19.200
Next, let's talk about the distance between&nbsp;
your native language and your target language.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:23.700
Your ability to quickly reach a certain&nbsp;
skill in your target language will greatly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:28.560
depend on how similar (or how different)&nbsp;
that language is from your mother tongue.

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:34.980
It's important to note here that languages can&nbsp;
differ in a wide variety of ways, often all at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:41.340
the same time. These differences include grammar,&nbsp;
pronunciation, intonation, word order, and more.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:45.420
If the two languages are similar&nbsp;
(like, say, English and Dutch),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:49.920
then great! Reaching B1 in three months is&nbsp;
actually quite a reasonable goal for you.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:53.400
But if the two languages are very&nbsp;
different (like, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:58.440
English and Japanese), even reaching a&nbsp;
B1 is unlikely to happen in three months.

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:02.940
Let's make these examples a bit more&nbsp;
concrete by taking one sentence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:05.760
and translating it across each of these languages.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:09.480
First, English and Dutch, which&nbsp;
are our two "close languages".

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:14.520
I heard that Amsterdam is a&nbsp;
fantastic city. I want to live there.&nbsp;

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:19.140
Ik hoorde dat Amsterdam een&nbsp;
fantastische stad is. Ik wil daar leven.

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:24.120
Looking at these sentences, it's possible&nbsp;
to determine that English and Dutch share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:29.940
similar vocabulary ("I heard" vs "Ik hoorde",&nbsp;
"I want to live there" vs "Ik wil daar leven").

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:34.080
You might also be able to tell that&nbsp;
they share a similar word order, too:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:38.760
I heard that: ik hoorde dat, I want&nbsp;
to live there vs Ik wil daar leven)

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:43.620
And these are just the beginning of all the&nbsp;
many similarities these two languages share.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:49.320
For example, they even compose words in similar&nbsp;
ways, and share a similar pronunciation, too!

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:55.620
Let's now move on to our more "distant"&nbsp;
pair of languages, English and Japanese:

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:58.200
I heard that Amsterdam is a fantastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:00.579
city. I want to live there.
アムステルダムは素晴らしい街だそうです。ぜひ住んでみたいですね 。&nbsp;

00:09:00.579 --> 00:09:07.320
amusuterudamu wa/ subarashii machi da&nbsp;
so-u desu. zehi sunde mitaii desu ne.

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:11.640
Looking at the word order, the differences&nbsp;
stand out right away. Translated word-by-word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:14.100
the Japanese sentence literally means “Amsterdam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:19.920
fantastic city to be I heard. I there&nbsp;
live want to”. Pretty different, huh?

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:24.480
And that's just the beginning. Japanese and&nbsp;
English have vastly different sound systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:30.780
grammar patterns, and intonation rules—not to&nbsp;
mention the completely different writing systems!

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:35.160
If you're an English speaker who wants to&nbsp;
learn Japanese, reaching even a B1 level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:40.380
is going to take you much, much longer&nbsp;
than 90 days. It can be done, for sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:45.120
but the time investment is typically on the scale&nbsp;
of a year or more, rather than a few months.

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:50.100
Let's move on to our last influential&nbsp;
factor for reaching a B1 in three months:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:53.820
your life circumstances, and how&nbsp;
favorable they are for language learning.

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:59.160
As with any skill, language learning is something&nbsp;
you get better at with time and experience. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.160 --> 00:10:03.780
you've never learned a language before, it might&nbsp;
take you months or years to reach a B1 level in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.780 --> 00:10:09.240
your first new language, but with every subsequent&nbsp;
language after that, things get easier and easier.

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:13.920
For that reason, an experienced language&nbsp;
learner will be much more likely to reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:18.960
conversational fluency in three months than a&nbsp;
complete newbie would. If you know what works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:23.700
you can start applying it right away, and&nbsp;
that leads to less wasted time overall.

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:27.840
Other aspects of your life can&nbsp;
make it easier or harder to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:31.380
languages too. In an earlier example,&nbsp;
I mentioned that you need to do a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:35.220
of daily learning to even hope to be&nbsp;
able to reach B1 in three months. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:39.120
person with a busy work or family life&nbsp;
probably won't be able to attempt that.

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:44.520
And then there's the matter of having access&nbsp;
to the language you're learning. If you're in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:48.180
a place where it's difficult to find natives to&nbsp;
practice with and good materials to learn from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:52.920
then the task of reaching conversational&nbsp;
fluency becomes way, way harder than it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:57.900
would otherwise be. On the other hand, if you're&nbsp;
literally IN a country where that language is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.900 --> 00:11:02.640
the dominant language, things suddenly become a&nbsp;
lot more manageable, even in short time frames.

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:06.660
Now that you understand some of the factors that&nbsp;
influence how fast a language can be learned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:09.840
you'll be better equipped to look at&nbsp;
your life and your target language&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:14.100
and determine what kind of language&nbsp;
goals are most reasonable for you.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:19.560
That's all I have for you today, but before we go,&nbsp;
let's do a quick recap of what we talked about:

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:24.480
To start, we raised a popular question: is&nbsp;
it possible to become fluent in three months?&nbsp;

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:28.560
Then, we talked about how the answer&nbsp;
depends on your definition of fluency.&nbsp;

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:33.240
If your definition of fluency is like&nbsp;
mine—close to a B2 level on the CEFR scale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:35.760
then the answer is no.
That being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:39.840
you certainly can still aim to achieve&nbsp;
a lot in a three-month period. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:44.520
I think reaching a B1 level (the level just&nbsp;
below B2) is certainly possible, so long as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:48.240
certain factors are in place that will help you&nbsp;
learn as quickly and efficiently as possible.&nbsp;

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:52.980
The first of these factors is your time on&nbsp;
task. If you can spend three to eight hours&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:56.340
each day on language learning, then you'll&nbsp;
have a good shot of reaching your goal.&nbsp;

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:59.880
The second of these three factors is&nbsp;
how close your target language is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:03.420
your native language. If they're very close&nbsp;
in structure, vocabulary, and pronunciation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:08.460
then learning quickly will be much easier.
The last of these factors is your set of life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:13.620
circumstances. If you've got lots of previous&nbsp;
language experience, lots of time, and lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:18.420
access to learning opportunities, then you'll&nbsp;
have the opportunity to learn quite quickly.

00:12:18.420 --> 00:12:22.560
Okay, that's all for this video. If&nbsp;
you'd like to learn more about how I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:26.760
personally would go about learning as much&nbsp;
language as possible within three months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:29.820
then check out the link in the description box!

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:33.900
Thanks for watching, and as&nbsp;
always: happy language learning!

